# BBSFirewall - Auto-block trigger patterns
#
# If a caller sends one of these strings within the first TRIGGER_SCAN_BYTES
# bytes, its IP is blacklisted (see TRIGGER_BLOCK_MODE: append to blocklist.txt,
# or a timed in-memory block). Applies to the telnet proxy and, in SSH terminate
# mode, to shell input and remote commands. Whitelisted IPs are never blocked.
#
# Pattern syntax, one per line:
#   plain text       case-insensitive substring match
#   /regex/flags     JavaScript regular expression (e.g. /^GET \/ HTTP/i)
#   escapes in plain patterns:  \r  \n  \t  \0  \xNN   (for binary probes)
# Lines starting with # are comments.
#
# WARNING: a real caller who types/sends one of these early would also be
# blocked. Keep the list to things a BBS client would never send. The protocol-
# mismatch entries below are safe; the shell/RCE ones are near-safe; review the
# "aggressive" block before enabling it.
#
# To use: copy to triggers.txt, set TRIGGER_LIST_PATH=./triggers.txt and
# TRIGGER_BLOCK_ENABLED=true in .env (or via the web config editor).

# --- wrong protocol on the telnet port (HTTP scanners, TLS probes, SSH probes) ---
GET /
POST /
HEAD /
PUT /
OPTIONS /
CONNECT
HTTP/1.
Host:
User-Agent:
\x16\x03\x01
\x16\x03\x03
SSH-2.0-
SSH-1.

# --- shell / RCE / injection probes ---
${jndi:
/bin/sh
/bin/bash
;chmod +x
| sh -c
$(id)
`id`
<?php
/etc/passwd
../../../
union select
' or '1'='1

# --- noisy binary padding some scanners lead with ---
\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00

# --- aggressive: also catch download-and-run tooling. Uncomment if you never
#     expect these words in the first bytes of a connection. ---
# wget http
# curl -O
# tftp -
# busybox
